    "stance-mutim-adi-loyalty": {
        title: "Enduring Loyalty to Al-Mut'im ibn 'Adi (A Polytheist Ally)",
        categoryBadge: "Lifelong Gratitude to Non-Muslim Benefactors",
        situation: "When the Prophet ﷺ returned bruised and bleeding from Taif, Al-Mut'im ibn 'Adi (a polytheist chieftain) armed his sons, surrounded the Prophet, and granted him public sanctuary to re-enter Makkah.",
        quote: "Years later after the Battle of Badr, looking upon seventy captured polytheist prisoners of war, the Prophet ﷺ declared: 'If Al-Mut'im ibn 'Adi were alive today and asked me for these repulsive captives, I would have gifted them all to him without any ransom!'",
        moralLesson: "Enduring gratitude that never forgets a favor, honoring honorable non-Muslim allies even years after their death.",
        proof: "Sahih Al-Bukhari (3139) from Jubayr ibn Mut'im",
        year: "2 AH (After Badr)",
        keyFigures: ["The Messenger of Allah ﷺ", "Al-Mut'im ibn 'Adi", "Jubayr ibn Mut'im"],
        detailedDialogue: "Al-Mut'im had commanded his eight armed sons: 'Put on your armor and stand at the pillars of the Kaaba!' He announced: 'O Quraysh, I have taken Muhammad under my protection.' The Prophet remembered this lifelong.",
        psychologicalAnalysis: "High emotional integrity: refusing to allow differences in creed to erase heartfelt gratitude for human solidarity shown during vulnerability.",
        contemporaryReflection: "Remembering and reciprocating kindness shown to us by non-Muslim colleagues, neighbors, and mentors throughout life.",
        practicalApplication: "1. Acknowledging favors received from others and thanking them publicly.\n2. Honoring the descendants of those who supported our families.\n3. Upholding loyalty across religious and political lines."
    },
    "stance-abu-jandal-treaty": {
        title: "Strict Treaty Fidelity: The Heartbreaking Test of Abu Jandal",
        categoryBadge: "Sacred Inviolability of International Treaties",
        situation: "Just as the ink was drying on the Treaty of Hudaybiyyah stipulating that Meccan defectors be returned, Abu Jandal—son of negotiator Suhayl ibn Amr—staggered into camp dragging his iron chains, having escaped torture in Makkah.",
        quote: "Suhayl struck his son across the face, shouting: 'Muhammad, this treaty is finalized before he arrived!' The Prophet pleaded: 'Pardon him for my sake.' Suhayl refused: 'Never!' The Prophet, with heart aching for the youth, turned to Abu Jandal: 'O Abu Jandal, be patient and seek reward; for Allah will surely make for you and the oppressed with you a way out. We have concluded a treaty, and we cannot betray our word.'",
        moralLesson: "The absolute sanctity of ratified international agreements; a state cannot breach its signed treaties even for emotional sympathy.",
        proof: "Sahih Al-Bukhari (2731) from Al-Miswar ibn Makhramah",
        year: "6 AH (Treaty of Hudaybiyyah)",
        keyFigures: ["The Messenger of Allah ﷺ", "Abu Jandal ibn Suhayl", "Suhayl ibn Amr", "Umar ibn al-Khattab"],
        detailedDialogue: "Abu Jandal cried: 'O Muslims! Shall I be returned to polytheists who torture me for my religion?' The scene crushed the companions' hearts, yet the Prophet's unbending integrity proved to the world that Islam's treaties are unbreakable covenants of honor.",
        psychologicalAnalysis: "Tragic moral conviction: enduring severe emotional heartbreak to preserve the supreme sanctity of sovereign covenants.",
        contemporaryReflection: "Honoring signed contracts, business pledges, and legal agreements strictly, even when sudden developments make them painful to fulfill.",
        practicalApplication: "1. Never breaking a signed contract for immediate personal benefit.\n2. Reassuring distressed allies while maintaining legal integrity.\n3. Seeking lawful, innovative solutions to protect the vulnerable."
    },
